  "raw": " China. The southwestern corner of East Asia historically played highly significant roles in developing Buddhism during the Tang period (618-907 AD), when monks from Vietnam and Phuiphong Buddhist sanctuaries brought Mahayana teachings to Nanjing\n\nAlthough both Daoism\nand Buddhism flourished in foreign territory even before Emperor\nWu Xi, founder of the Five Dynasties era, conquered Korea around 571 BC , much of ancient Buddhism comes into play after his reign. With the rise of early Polytheistic religions which mingled (at least partially, some would later believe) with Chinese customs and beliefs, including those brought by the travels of earlier Zen masters such as Shen Xuan (563 BC - 493 BC ) and Guo Hui Tie (DAQ 256 - 432BC ), it can be said that at least as well established was Buddhism in Japan as it is in China In addition , there were floodings of Buddhist monks from India , Assam , Tibet , and Han H",
  "serialized": "China. The southwestern corner of East Asia historically played highly significant roles in developing Buddhism during the Tang period (618-907 AD), when monks from Vietnam and Phuiphong Buddhist sanctuaries brought Mahayana teachings to Nanjing Although both Daoism and Buddhism flourished in foreign territory even before Emperor Wu Xi, founder of the Five Dynasties era, conquered Korea around 571 BC , much of ancient Buddhism comes into play after his reign. With the rise of early Polytheistic religions which mingled (at least partially, some would later believe) with Chinese customs and beliefs, including those brought by the travels of earlier Zen masters such as Shen Xuan (563 BC - 493 BC ) and Guo Hui Tie (DAQ 256 - 432BC ), it can be said that at least as well established was Buddhism in Japan as it is in China In addition , there were floodings of Buddhist monks from India , Assam , Tibet , and Han H",
